From 0b6f7c7fd9465e6c439fb14890512576d2151095 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Job Noorman <jnoorman@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 16:49:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] ir3/sched: allow a0.x/a1.x writes to be scheduled earlier

We currently don't allow such writes to scheduled if the current address
register has unscheduled users, unless necessary to break a cycle. It
turns out that allowing them to be scheduled earlier can drastically
reduce register pressure in some cases.

For example, preambles often contain many sequential sequences like
this:

mova1 ...
isam.v ..., a1.x
; process SSBO values
mova1 ...
stc c[a1.x] ; store processed SSBO values

If the a1.x value is the same for all isam.v but different for stc, we
might end up with a huge register pressure because all the SSBO values
pile up. Allowing the a1.x value needed by stc to be scheduled earlier,
gives the scheduler the opportunity to reduce register pressure.

This commit allows address writes to be scheduled earlier and splitting
the current address register when that happens. Scheduling address
writes too early is prevented by 1) making should_defer return true for
them and 2) not allowing them to be scheduled before a certain number of
users has been scheduled first.

